1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,280
In this lecture, we'll talk about control hazards and exceptions.

2
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:14,280
Control Dependents will complete our discussion of pipeline hazards.

3
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:19,760
We'll discuss what control hazards are, different ways of resolving them, and then finally wrap

4
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:25,240
up with exception handling.

5
00:00:25,240 --> 00:00:29,280
First let's define what we mean by program control dependence.

6
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:35,560
An instruction Y is control dependent on an instruction X if X determines whether or not

7
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:37,600
Y will be executed.

8
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:41,120
And this is specifically due to conditional branches.

9
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:48,600
A branch instruction needs to be executed to see if it will or will not be taken and

10
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:52,080
figure out which instruction to fetch next.

11
00:00:52,080 --> 00:01:00,520
Many instructions that follow the branch are control dependent on it.

12
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:06,840
Here's an example program with a pair of branches with control dependence listed.

13
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:11,560
Instruction 0 is branch not equal with the target of I3.

14
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:17,680
This means instructions 1 and 2 are executed if and only if this branch is not taken, which

15
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:21,680
makes them control dependent on instruction 0.

16
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:28,640
Since the branch not equal targets I3, this means instructions 3 or 4 are not control dependent

17
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:36,120
on instruction 0 since they are executed regardless of the values in S1 and S3.

18
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:38,820
Instruction 4 is a branch equal instruction.

19
00:01:38,820 --> 00:01:44,320
If the branch is taken, then the PC jumps back to instruction 0, which means instructions

20
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:51,080
0 through 4 are all control dependent on that branch.

21
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:59,320
Technically, control dependence doesn't need to be preserved to ensure program correctness.

22
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:03,880
It's okay for us to execute some instructions that shouldn't have been executed as long

23
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:12,840
as those speculative instructions still preserve data dependencies and don't cause new exceptions.

24
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:17,680
A control hazard occurs when an incorrect instruction is fetched into the pipeline because

25
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:22,180
the outcome of a branch instruction has not yet been resolved.

26
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:26,160
This is possible in our five-stage pipeline because branches don't get resolved until

27
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:30,120
a later stage.

28
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:35,160
In our base implementation shown here, the branch's decision is generated in the memory

29
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:36,160
stage.

30
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:40,760
Specifically, the PC will be updated to the correct instruction address after the branch's

31
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:46,920
memory stage.

32
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:50,440
Let's explore control hazards with a toy example.

33
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:56,600
Say that this branch equal instruction will be taken because T0 and T1 are indeed equal.

34
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:00,760
The issue is that we don't know if it's taken until the end of cycle 4 when the branch

35
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:04,680
instruction has finished the memory stage.

36
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:10,120
Our processor kept updating PC to PC plus 4 and three additional instructions were fetched

37
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:17,240
and pushed into the pipeline before the branch was resolved.

38
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:21,000
Next we'll discuss the ways we can resolve control hazards.

39
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,760
The first and simplest solution is to continue to fetch the next instruction, that is PC

40
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:29,120
plus 4, until the branch is resolved.

41
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:34,120
This is effectively predicting and hoping that the branch is not taken.

42
00:03:34,120 --> 00:03:40,680
However, if the branch is to be taken, we need to flush or turn into no ops, these instructions

43
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:45,600
that we incorrectly fetch before they change any program visible state, like data memory

44
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:48,420
or the register file.

45
00:03:48,420 --> 00:03:53,040
This is shown in the pipeline diagram as equal signs in cycle 5.

46
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:57,920
In this case, flushing looks like we instead chose to stall three cycles.

47
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:02,720
This approach is a form of speculation where we intentionally execute instructions before

48
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:07,440
we've confirmed that they should be executed to improve performance.

49
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:12,360
That means that if most branches are not taken most of the time, then the performance loss

50
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:19,280
that happens when they are is minimized.

51
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:23,600
To reduce the performance overhead, let's left shift the branch resolution as early

52
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:24,940
as possible.

53
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:30,760
This leverages the fact that in our MIPS ISA, we only need to support simple equality comparisons

54
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:35,320
in our branches, so they don't affect the cycle time by much.

55
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:42,320
We can choose to resolve branches at the execute or even in the decode stage, as shown here.

56
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:46,800
Let's first compute the branch target PC in the decode stage.

57
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:50,620
This is simple since the target only depends on the instruction word, which was already

58
00:04:50,620 --> 00:04:52,920
available at that stage.

59
00:04:52,920 --> 00:04:57,120
We will have to instantiate an adder as we can no longer use the existing one in the

60
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:59,360
ALU.

61
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:05,160
The comparison adds a bit of delay since it's a combinatorial logic cloud that comes after

62
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:09,880
the end of the decode stage, which is after the gate delay of reading registers out of

63
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:15,200
the register file in the second phase of the clock due to register bypass.

64
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:20,200
Depending on the comparison type, the result of the comparison is effectively the PC source

65
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:27,160
control signal that selects between PC plus 4 and the branch target for the next PC.

66
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:29,960
The last thing we must attend to is the flush.

67
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:35,440
If the branch is taken as determined by the control logic, then the IFAD pipeline register

68
00:05:35,440 --> 00:05:40,840
must be cleared as the instruction currently in the fetch stage has been incorrectly fetched.

69
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:48,040
We must propagate a NOOP instead of the incorrect instruction.

70
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:50,520
Let's go back to our earlier example.

71
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:55,240
Now the branch is resolved in cycle 2 when the branch equal instruction is at the decode

72
00:05:55,240 --> 00:05:58,160
stage.

73
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:02,760
This only needs to flush one instruction instead of three, which is the load instruction, which

74
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:10,360
gets cleared upon entering the decode stage in cycle 3.

75
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:13,480
Here's an animation of how the control hazard is resolved.

76
00:06:13,480 --> 00:06:18,440
We start in cycle 2 where we fetch the load instruction speculatively, predicting that

77
00:06:18,440 --> 00:06:21,600
the branch will not be taken.

78
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:25,960
But if it is taken, then the load instruction gets converted to a NOOP in the next cycle

79
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:30,400
and the correct subtract instruction is then fetched.

80
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:41,480
The NOOP then propagates through the rest of the pipeline as if the load never happened.

81
00:06:41,480 --> 00:06:43,680
But now what about raw hazards?

82
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:48,600
Unlike other instructions, we now need the register operands in the decode stage for

83
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:53,200
branch instruction, which was previously only needed in the ALU stage, which is why

84
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:59,120
we created the execute to execute forwarding and memory to execute forwarding.

85
00:06:59,120 --> 00:07:03,960
In this example, register T1 is read by the branch equal instruction, but it's not ready

86
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:09,280
until after the add executes.

87
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:13,800
One solution is to stall and implement full forwarding back to the decode stage.

88
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:19,120
At cycle 3, the branch needs to stall since execute is still working on the add.

89
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:23,400
The add's results will be ready in cycle 4 and can be forwarded to the comparison logic

90
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:25,880
in the branch's decode stage.

91
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:29,920
This is an example of execute to decode forwarding.

92
00:07:29,920 --> 00:07:37,720
Memory to decode forwarding can be implemented in a similar way.

93
00:07:37,720 --> 00:07:42,560
Some early architectures, including MIPS and digital signal processors, had what's known

94
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:48,240
as a branch delay slot instead of adding hardware complexity for resolving control hazards.

95
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:53,480
A branch delay slot is an instruction immediately after a branch that is unconditionally executed

96
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:57,200
regardless of whether or not the branch is taken.

97
00:07:57,200 --> 00:08:02,280
This puts the onus on the software to handle control hazards with legal instructions instead

98
00:08:02,280 --> 00:08:05,880
of imposing hardware complexity.

99
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:10,360
In the example program shown, the add instruction is currently in the delay slot.

100
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:15,240
This means that whether or not the branch is taken, the add will be executed to completion

101
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:19,560
and will never be flushed.

102
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:24,440
In this example, the add instruction in the branch delay slot is illegal because it violates

103
00:08:24,440 --> 00:08:30,280
program correctance by introducing a false data dependence on registered T5.

104
00:08:30,280 --> 00:08:34,280
If the branch were taken, then the subtract instruction is supposed to read a different

105
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:39,120
value of T5, not the one produced by the add, which wouldn't be executed in the original

106
00:08:39,160 --> 00:08:41,840
program.

107
00:08:41,840 --> 00:08:46,400
We have two options for filling the branch delay slots.

108
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:51,360
The first is to insert a no op into the program at the branch delay slot.

109
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:55,760
No ops don't affect program state and thus are always safe to insert.

110
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:59,840
With this, the add instruction will only be executed when the branch is not taken, which

111
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:04,560
is the correct program behavior.

112
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:08,160
The better option would be to fill the branch delay slot with an instruction that needed

113
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:10,240
to be executed anyway.

114
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:13,120
This avoids the loss cycle due to the no op.

115
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:18,280
In this example, we can move the load that preceded the branch equal to after, as the

116
00:09:18,280 --> 00:09:24,120
load did not write any registers that the branch equal depended on reading.

117
00:09:24,120 --> 00:09:29,120
This program would produce correct results whether the branch were taken or not.

118
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:36,120
A common approach to resolving control hazards is dynamic branch prediction.

119
00:09:36,840 --> 00:09:41,840
This is implemented in most modern processors, including the ones in your tablet, laptop,

120
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:46,320
or phone that you're probably watching this video on.

121
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:50,400
The idea is to predict the branch outcome or direction, which is either taken or not

122
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:51,760
taken.

123
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:55,960
This prediction is typically employed in the fetch stage, which is cycle one in the pipeline

124
00:09:55,960 --> 00:10:00,960
diagram, so that upon the next cycle, the subtract instruction can be immediately fetched

125
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:04,800
if the branch is predicted to be taken.

126
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:09,360
Obviously the branch target address is also either computed earlier or predicted in some

127
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:13,880
way using branch target buffers, but this is a more advanced topic that we won't be

128
00:10:13,880 --> 00:10:15,880
covering in this course.

129
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:21,040
So in this example, by the end of cycle two, we will know whether or not the branch is

130
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:22,800
indeed taken.

131
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:29,800
In this case, our prediction is correct, so we don't have to do anything else.

132
00:10:30,680 --> 00:10:34,680
If our branch predictor instead mispredicts thinking that the branch won't be taken,

133
00:10:34,680 --> 00:10:40,760
then after cycle two, the add instruction will be flushed as usual.

134
00:10:40,760 --> 00:10:47,760
In the end, we'd have the same single cycle penalty as in the previous predict.taken approach.

135
00:10:50,440 --> 00:10:55,880
The simplest way to implement branch direction prediction is to use a branch history table

136
00:10:55,880 --> 00:10:59,600
or BHT with one-bit predictions.

137
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:04,240
Bits of the current PC, which is the address of the branch instruction itself, are used

138
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:08,440
to index into the BHT and select an entry.

139
00:11:08,440 --> 00:11:14,760
In this example, an 8-bit BHT is indexed by three bits of the PC.

140
00:11:14,760 --> 00:11:20,600
As an aside, for MIPS, we typically ignore the two least significant bits of the PC.

141
00:11:20,600 --> 00:11:25,520
Why is that?

142
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:30,280
So now once we've selected an entry in the BHT, each entry contains a one-bit prediction

143
00:11:30,280 --> 00:11:32,760
to indicate taken or not taken.

144
00:11:32,760 --> 00:11:38,120
The bit is then updated according to the FSM or finite state machine on the right.

145
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:41,120
