new IPC.

234
00:18:39,400 --> 00:18:43,720
In the first option, branches are resolved in the decode stage so we only have a one

235
00:18:43,720 --> 00:18:47,840
cycle flush penalty when we mispredict.

236
00:18:47,840 --> 00:18:53,400
Since 30% of the instructions are branches and 60 of them will incur this one cycle penalty,

237
00:18:53,400 --> 00:19:06,840
we get a new CPI of 1.38 which at 300 microseconds results in 2,415 instructions per second.

238
00:19:06,840 --> 00:19:14,080
For option 2, we only have a one cycle flush penalty on 10% of our branches barely bumping

239
00:19:14,080 --> 00:19:17,920
our CPI to 1.23.

240
00:19:17,920 --> 00:19:22,400
If we calculate the new wall clock time for an instruction, we can see that we can process

241
00:19:22,400 --> 00:19:29,800
2,501 instructions per second or 86 more instructions than the basic predictor.

242
00:19:29,800 --> 00:19:35,840
If your crucial workloads have 30% branches, the branch predictor should be one place that's

243
00:19:35,840 --> 00:19:43,000
high on your list to research.

244
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,400
Now let's review branch delay slots.

245
00:19:45,400 --> 00:19:49,040
Given this program, we put a no-op in the branch delay slot.

246
00:19:49,040 --> 00:19:52,200
We prefer to keep our processor fully utilized.

247
00:19:52,200 --> 00:19:55,460
Which of these instructions are legal to replace the no-op with?

248
00:19:55,460 --> 00:20:02,560
You could ignore potential exceptions for this exercise.

249
00:20:02,560 --> 00:20:05,760
Does this reordering result in a legal substitution?

250
00:20:05,760 --> 00:20:10,360
Pause the video and reason about any data hazards.

251
00:20:10,360 --> 00:20:17,120
Yeah, this is legal.

252
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:22,680
The branch on equal doesn't read the subtracts result and it needs to be executed anyway

253
00:20:22,680 --> 00:20:27,760
so the program would still produce the same result.

254
00:20:27,760 --> 00:20:29,760
How about the NOR instruction?

255
00:20:29,760 --> 00:20:33,040
Does here and look for hazards?

256
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:40,880
Nope, this is not legal since there was a raw dependence on register S1 between the

257
00:20:40,880 --> 00:20:43,800
NOR and branch equal instruction.

258
00:20:43,800 --> 00:20:48,560
The branch needs the new value of S1 for its comparison and without this the program wouldn't

259
00:20:48,560 --> 00:20:56,600
produce the same results.

260
00:20:56,600 --> 00:20:59,280
How about the add instruction after the branch?

261
00:20:59,280 --> 00:21:02,320
Can we shift that up into the branch delay slot?

262
00:21:02,320 --> 00:21:06,320
Pause here.

263
00:21:06,320 --> 00:21:12,400
And no, this is also not legal.

264
00:21:12,400 --> 00:21:17,840
In the original program, the add was only executed if the branch was not taken.

265
00:21:17,840 --> 00:21:21,520
Since it's now in a branch delay slot, it will get executed every time.

266
00:21:21,520 --> 00:21:23,400
Also, it gets worse.

267
00:21:23,400 --> 00:21:29,240
If the branch is taken, T1 now reads a different value of S1 in the OR instruction because

268
00:21:29,240 --> 00:21:37,320
the add instruction was executed.

269
00:21:37,320 --> 00:21:38,560
Okay, last one.

270
00:21:38,560 --> 00:21:39,760
How about this?

271
00:21:39,760 --> 00:21:44,000
Can we move the OR from T1 up to the main line?

272
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:51,760
Please pause again.

273
00:21:51,760 --> 00:21:53,360
Actually, yes, this is legal.

274
00:21:53,360 --> 00:21:58,360
This creates a right after right anti-dependence hazard because if we do not take the branch,

275
00:21:58,640 --> 00:22:02,880
then add is going to overwrite S4 anyway.

276
00:22:02,880 --> 00:22:09,720
In a way, this means that OR is a know-op if we do not branch, but it's useful work

277
00:22:09,720 --> 00:22:11,280
if we do.

278
00:22:11,280 --> 00:22:14,120
So it's not a win for both branch possibilities.

